            <ArticleTitle>Intracranial hypertension secondary to a skull lesion without mass effect.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>72-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jocn.2015.12.035</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0967-5868(16)30021-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report and discuss five patients with intracranial hypertension due to a skull lesion reducing cerebral sinus patency with a compressive, non-thrombotic mechanism. We illustrate the importance of a high level of suspicion for this condition in patients presenting with headache, papilledema and increased intracranial pressure in the absence of focal signs or radiological evidence of mass effect.</AbstractText>
